mock-data-generator/node_modules/*
sample/*
.vscode/*
mock-data-generator/markers/*
batch-app/markers/*
/.idea/
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo test-suite generated outputs (finding MI-06).
# WHY: these paths are PRODUCED by the runner scripts (scripts/build_test_*.sh,
# scripts/run_*.sh) and must never be committed. The policy is intentionally
# NARROW -- it names only generated artifacts, so it cannot mask a real source
# file. The mutable per-run test WORKSPACE and its loaded VSAM INDEX files are
# already created OUTSIDE the repo (under $TMPDIR by scripts/test_env.sh, per
# finding MA-04), so no workspace/index rule is needed here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiled COBOL programs / shared modules (CARDDEMO_BUILD_DIR default).
/build/
# JUnit XML + coverage reports (CARDDEMO_REPORTS_DIR default).
/reports/
# gcov line-coverage artifacts from the GnuCOBOL-transpiled C (--coverage build).
# Kept global (not just under /build/) so a manual gcov run in a source tree is
# also covered; the GnuCOBOL --save-temps intermediates themselves are written
# into CARDDEMO_BUILD_DIR (/build/, already ignored above), so no separate .c
# rule is needed here.
*.gcno
*.gcda
*.gcov
# Python-harness coverage data (coverage.py; parallel mode writes .coverage.*).
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
# pytest / Python bytecode caches.
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CardDemo AWS migration -- generated build output, IaC state, local env.
# WHY: the migration adds four build trees (services/, ui/, data-migration/,
# infra/) whose toolchains -- Maven, npm/Vite, Terraform and CPython -- all
# write generated bytes back INTO the working tree, and none of it is source.
# The MI-06 policy above is carried over unchanged: this set is intentionally
# NARROW, naming only generated artifacts and secret-bearing local files, so
# it cannot mask a real source file. The state and env rules carry the extra
# weight. Database and Cognito seed-user passwords are generated at
# `terraform apply` time by the hashicorp/random provider and are recorded in
# Terraform state as PLAINTEXT, so one committed state file would disclose the
# entire credential set at once. Making that structurally impossible is what
# satisfies the project's "no secrets committed to the repository" constraint,
# rather than trusting a reviewer to catch it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Maven build output; each of the nine services/ modules gets its own target/.
# Assumption: deliberately NOT root-anchored, unlike /build/ and /reports/
# above, because a target/ only ever appears nested at services/<module>/ and
# never at the repository root, where a leading slash would match nothing.
target/

# npm dependency tree installed for the React SPA under ui/.
# Assumption: the legacy `mock-data-generator/node_modules/*` rule at the top
# of this file is PATH-SCOPED to a tree this repository no longer carries, so
# it does not cover ui/ -- this unanchored rule is required, not redundant.
node_modules/

# Vite production bundle for the SPA, written by `npm run build` to ui/dist/.
# Assumption: left unanchored for the same reason as target/ above -- the
# bundle lands under ui/, never at a root-level dist/.
dist/

# Provider and module cache created by `terraform init` (infra/bootstrap/,
# infra/envs/dev/, infra/envs/prod/, plus any infra/modules/* inited locally).
# Alternatives Considered: ignoring all of infra/ was evaluated and rejected
# outright -- the .tf source under infra/ IS the deliverable, so a blanket rule
# would mask the very files the migration exists to ship. Naming only the
# generated cache directory keeps the promise the MI-06 block makes above.
.terraform/

# Assumption: .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored. The rule above
# ends in a slash, so it matches the cache DIRECTORY only; the lock file is a
# sibling regular file and stays tracked on purpose, because it pins provider
# checksums and is what makes `terraform init` reproducible in CI and for
# every other operator.

# Terraform state, plus every backup and workspace-suffixed variant of it.
# Trade-off: the second pattern is deliberately wider than a bare
# `terraform.tfstate` so that terraform.tfstate.backup and
# terraform.tfstate.<serial>.backup are caught as well. That extra breadth is
# worth accepting because the failure it prevents is disclosure of every
# credential the random provider generated, in plaintext, in a single file --
# while its cost is nil, since no tracked file here ends in .tfstate or
# .tfstate.<anything>.
# Alternatives Considered: writing this as the one glob `*.tfstate*` covers the
# same files in a single line, but its trailing `*` spans the dot too, so it
# also swallows unrelated names such as `x.tfstateignore`. The explicit pair
# matches the state file and its dot-suffixed variants and nothing else.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*

# Assumption: *.tfvars is deliberately NOT ignored, even though ignoring tfvars
# is the usual Terraform convention. Here infra/envs/dev/terraform.tfvars and
# infra/envs/prod/terraform.tfvars are required deliverables carrying only
# sizing, capacity and retention parameters and never a secret value, so a
# *.tfvars rule would silently drop two files this migration has to ship.

# Local environment overrides for the SPA and the ETL. Real values live only in
# AWS Secrets Manager and SSM Parameter Store and are read at startup, so an
# .env on a developer's disk is untracked scratch by definition.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Assumption: ui/.env.example is a required deliverable -- it documents variable
# NAMES with no values -- and has to stay tracked. The three rules above are
# narrow enough that none of them matches `.env.example` to begin with; these
# negations exist purely as a guard should anyone later widen them to `.env.*`.
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Refactoring Rationale: `__pycache__/` and `*.py[cod]` are deliberately NOT
# repeated in this block. Both already exist in the MI-06 block above and both
# are unanchored, so they already cover the new data-migration/ package; a
# second copy would duplicate a rule without changing any behaviour.
